[[@bengaluru|Bengaluru]] gets most of its water from the Kaveri river, about 100 km away. The city sits high on a plateau, so the water has to be pumped hundreds of metres uphill. Mumbai draws water from a chain of lakes and reservoirs in the hills to the north, filled each year by the [[@monsoon|monsoon]].

New York City gets most of its water from reservoirs in the Catskill Mountains and the Delaware River area, well over 100 km away. Much of it flows to the city by [[gravity|gravity]] through long [[@tunnels|tunnels]] called [[aqueduct|aqueducts]]. The water is so clean at the source that most of it doesn't need filtering, only disinfecting.

{{story:water-purify}}

The biggest shark ever was [[@megalodon|Megalodon]]. From its teeth, some as long as your hand, scientists estimate it grew to around 15 to 18 m, longer than a school bus. It probably hunted whales.

Megalodon died out about 3.6 million years ago. The seas were cooling, and its prey was changing. Some scientists think the great white shark, a smaller and nimbler rival, may have added to its troubles.

{{story:great-dying}}

Escape velocity does not depend on what you throw. A pebble and the space station need the same speed. Heavier things take more energy to get there, but the number is the same. That's the same reason a feather and a hammer fall together on the Moon, where there is no air to slow the feather. [[@gravity-fall|Gravity]] pulls harder on heavier things, but they are also harder to move, and the two effects cancel out.

What matters is the planet. The bigger and heavier it is, the higher its escape velocity. On the Moon it is only about 2.4 km per second, which is why the Apollo astronauts could blast off from it with a small rocket. On Jupiter, it is about 60 km per second.

{{story:jules-verne}}

In his late twenties, Beethoven began to hear buzzing and whistling in his ears, and to lose his hearing. It got slowly worse for the rest of his life. Doctors tried many treatments, but none worked. Scientists still don't know what caused it.

For a musician, this was terrifying. In 1802, staying in the village of Heiligenstadt near Vienna, he wrote a letter to his brothers about his despair. But in the same letter, he said he could not leave the world until he had written all the music he felt inside him.

{{story:ears-hear}}

Chills tend to come at surprising or powerful moments. A singer jumps to a high note. A quiet passage suddenly bursts into a [[@orchestra|full orchestra]]. A new instrument enters, or the harmony shifts in an unexpected direction. In a cricket stadium, it might be thousands of people singing the same line together.

Film composers know this well. A soundtrack often swells just as the hero wins, and in a dark cinema hall you can almost feel the audience shiver. In Indian classical music, a long, slow build in a [[@raga-time|raga]] can end in a fast, dazzling passage that makes listeners gasp and call out "wah".

{{story:goosebumps}}

Unlike many [[@indian-classical-dance|classical dance]]-dramas, Yakshagana actors talk. After the Bhagavata sings a verse, an actor might argue, joke, boast or plead, in Kannada, sometimes for many minutes. The best actors are admired for their quick wit and knowledge of the epics as much as for their dancing.

That is why no two shows are the same. The story stays fixed, but each actor finds new things to say. A comic character, the *hasyagara*, often teases the audience and other actors, bringing laughter into even the most serious tale.

{{story:mahabharata}}

When Gandhi launched the [[@non-cooperation|Non-Cooperation Movement]] in 1920, he asked Indians to boycott British schools and colleges. Thousands of students walked out. New national schools and universities, such as Jamia Millia Islamia and Gujarat Vidyapith, were founded for them.

During the Quit India Movement of 1942, students again led protests, strikes and marches. Some of the youngest freedom fighters were teenagers. In Odisha, a 12-year-old boat boy named Baji Rout was shot in 1938 when he refused to ferry British-Indian police across a river. He is remembered as one of India's youngest martyrs.

{{story:anne-frank}}

A touchdown is worth 6 points. Afterwards the team can try an easy kick for 1 extra point, or run or pass into the end zone again for 2. A field goal, kicked through the posts from anywhere on the field, is worth 3. If the defense tackles an attacker in his own end zone, it scores a safety, worth 2.

It is a bit like an over in cricket. Each down is one delivery, and the team must get enough out of its four before the chance passes.

{{story:brain-works}}

The fabric is denim, a tough cotton cloth with a diagonal weave. The name probably comes from "serge de Nîmes", a fabric from the town of Nîmes in France. The word "jeans" is linked to Genoa in Italy, where sailors wore trousers of a similar cloth.

Denim is made from [[@clothes-cotton|cotton]]. Blue threads run along the length and white threads run across. The weave pushes the blue threads to the outside, which is why jeans are pale inside.

{{story:clothes-cotton}}

In 1877, Thomas Edison in New Jersey built the phonograph. He shouted into a mouthpiece, and a needle pressed a groove into tinfoil wrapped around a turning cylinder. When he ran the needle back along the groove, the machine spoke back: "Mary had a little lamb."

Ten years later, Emile Berliner invented the gramophone, which used flat discs instead of cylinders. Discs could be stamped out by the thousand from one master copy, which made records cheap enough for ordinary families.

{{story:sound-travel}}

Deep in your brain, an area called the brainstem keeps watch on your body. When you start moving, nerves send signals to the heart's [[@accident-pacemaker|natural pacemaker]], a small patch of cells that sets its [[rhythm|rhythm]].

[[sensor|Sensors]] in your blood vessels notice rising carbon dioxide, and your brain responds by raising both heart rate and breathing. A [[hormone|hormone]] called adrenaline, released when you're excited or scared, makes the heart beat faster too. That is why your heart can race before a race has even started.

{{story:sports-science}}

Goodyear's own story goes like this. One winter day in 1839, in Woburn, Massachusetts, a lump of rubber mixed with sulphur touched a hot stove. Instead of melting, it charred at the edge like leather. Just inside the burnt rim was a firm, springy band of rubber that stayed bendy even when he nailed it outside in the frost.

It took him years more to get the heat and timing right. He patented the process in the USA in 1844.

{{story:inventions-accidents}}

The US flag has 13 red and white stripes for the [[@usa-statehood|13 colonies]] that declared independence from Britain in 1776. The 50 white stars on blue stand for today's 50 states. A new star is added whenever a state joins, and the current design has been used since 1960, after Hawaii became a state.

The colours have no official meaning in law. In 1782, when Charles Thomson described the Great Seal, he wrote that white meant purity and innocence, red meant hardiness and valour, and blue meant vigilance, perseverance and justice. People later applied those words to the flag.

{{story:colour-meaning}}

At Puri, on the coast, stands the temple of Jagannath, [[@myths-krishna-childhood|a form of Krishna]] worshipped with his brother Balabhadra and sister Subhadra. Every year, usually in June or July, the three deities are brought out and placed on enormous wooden chariots, rebuilt from fresh timber each year.

Hundreds of thousands of people pull the chariots by thick ropes through the streets to another temple a few kilometres away. The English word "juggernaut", meaning a huge, unstoppable force, comes from Jagannath and these giant chariots.

{{story:sea-turtles}}

In June, Congress chose five men to draft a declaration. They gave the writing to [[@thomas-jefferson|Thomas Jefferson]], a 33-year-old from Virginia. He worked in two rented rooms in the house of a bricklayer, Jacob Graff, writing on a small portable desk he had designed himself.

[[@benjamin-franklin|Benjamin Franklin]] and John Adams suggested changes. Congress then cut and edited it further, removing about a quarter of the words. One passage blaming the British king for the slave trade was taken out.

{{story:us-constitution}}

Spoken languages and sign languages can say anything. You can talk about the past, the future, what might have happened and what never will. You can make up a sentence nobody has ever said, and others still understand it.

Emoji struggle with that. Try writing "I would have come if my aunt had not called" in emoji. There is no emoji for "would have" or "if". Studies of real messages show most people use emoji alongside words, usually one or two at a time, to add feeling. Long chains of emoji with no words are rare, and often turn into a guessing game.

{{story:hieroglyphs}}

A single bee makes only a tiny amount of honey in her life, about a twelfth of a teaspoon. A strong hive can still make many kilograms a year, because it may have tens of thousands of workers.

To make one jar, bees must visit hundreds of thousands of flowers, often flying several kilometres from the hive. They find the best patches using a waggle dance that tells their sisters the direction and distance, explained in [[@bees-dance|how bees dance]]. The cells they fill are near-perfect [[@geometry-everyday|hexagons]], a shape that holds the most honey for the least wax, as you can see in [[@honeycomb|the maths of the honeycomb]].

{{story:honeycomb}}

In Korea, seaweed soup called miyeok-guk is eaten on birthdays and by mothers after a baby is born. Korean gim, toasted with sesame oil and salt, is a popular snack. In Japan, a big brown seaweed called kombu is simmered to make dashi, the savoury stock behind many dishes.

Far from Asia, people in Wales boil a seaweed called laver for hours into a dark paste called laverbread. It is fried and eaten with breakfast.

{{story:kelp-forests}}

In the 1880s, the USA charged a tax on imported vegetables but not on fruit. A [[@new-york|New York]] family of food importers named Nix argued that their tomatoes were fruit and should not be taxed. The tax collector, Edward Hedden, disagreed.

The case reached the [[@supreme-courts|Supreme Court]]. In 1893, the judges ruled unanimously that tomatoes should be taxed as vegetables. They accepted that botanists called them fruits. But the law used everyday language, and in everyday life tomatoes were served with the main meal. So the importers paid.

{{story:supreme-courts}}
